Adding a **second failure mode** from the same root cause that isn't in the original report: the flag doesn't just get set to the wrong value, it can get **deleted outright** while caveman is still active.

`.caveman-active.prev` (`caveman-mode-tracker.js:19`) is global too. A one-shot independent mode (`/caveman-commit`, `/caveman-review`, `/caveman-compress`) writes `commit` to the flag and stashes the prior prose mode in `.prev`. On the next ordinary prompt the restore path runs (`caveman-mode-tracker.js:179-190`):

```js
if (activeMode && INDEPENDENT_MODES.has(activeMode) && !setIndependentThisTurn) {
  const prev = readFlag(prevPath);
  try { fs.unlinkSync(prevPath); } catch (e) {}
  if (prev && !INDEPENDENT_MODES.has(prev)) {
    ...
  } else {
    recordModeChange(claudeDir, null);
    try { fs.unlinkSync(flagPath); } catch (e) {}   // ← badge gone
    activeMode = null;
  }
}
```

If a concurrent session has meanwhile overwritten or consumed the global `.prev`, `prev` is falsy and the flag is removed. Caveman is still on from the user's point of view, but the statusline renders nothing.

Worth noting this isn't only cosmetic: the per-turn reinforcement at `caveman-mode-tracker.js:193` reads the same flag, so once it's deleted the ruleset stops being injected and the model drifts back to verbose mid-session. Cross-session clobbering has the same effect — a session started elsewhere changes the rules injected into a session already running.

Evidence from `~/.claude/.caveman-mode-log.jsonl`, one machine, two concurrent sessions:

```
07-30 08:50:31      full -> ultra     user ran /caveman ultra
07-30 09:01:22     ultra -> full      second session started
07-30 09:03:03      full -> ultra     user ran /caveman ultra again
07-30 09:04:03     ultra -> full      second session started again
07-30 09:14:23      full -> commit    /caveman-commit
07-30 09:19:01     ultra -> None      flag deleted
07-30 09:21:54      None -> commit    /caveman-commit
07-30 09:22:56    commit -> None      flag deleted
```

No `.caveman-active` file existed afterwards while caveman was still expected to be active.

The `session_id` fix proposed above would resolve this too, as long as `.caveman-active.prev` gets keyed by session in the same change — otherwise the restore path keeps racing across sessions.

